<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Gompers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Gompers was a British-born American cigar maker and trade union leader who founded the American Federation of Labor and was its president, but for one year, from 1886 until his death. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>'s East End, he emigrated with his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1863 and joined the cigar makers' union the next year. He favoured organisation and collective bargaining to win shorter hours and higher wages. In 1919 he chaired the Commission on International Labour Legislation, whose recommendations for a workers' rights charter entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-gompers.jpg" alt="Samuel Gompers" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James E. Purdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Gompers_cph.3a02952.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
